# {{.Name}} ({{.Type}})

The virtual machine resource allows you to create and manage virtual machines within your [Latitude.sh](https://latitude.sh/) account. A common use case is a small VM that acts as an SSH bastion / OpenVPN endpoint.

## Example usage

```hcl
resource "latitudesh_ssh_key" "ssh_key" {
  name       = "bastion-key"
  public_key = "ssh-ed25519 AAAA..."
}

resource "latitudesh_virtual_machine" "bastion" {
  name             = "bastion"
  plan             = "vm-small"
  billing          = "hourly" # hourly, monthly (default) or yearly (reserved projects)
  operating_system = "ubuntu_24_04_x64_lts"
  ssh_keys         = [latitudesh_ssh_key.ssh_key.id]
}

output "bastion_ip" {
  value = latitudesh_virtual_machine.bastion.primary_ipv4
}
```

The resource waits for the virtual machine to reach a running state with a primary IPv4 address before completing, so `primary_ipv4` is usable by dependent resources in the same apply.

> **Note:** The virtual machine create API does not accept `user_data`/cloud-init. Bootstrapping (for example installing OpenVPN) is performed out-of-band after the VM is created, e.g. over SSH.

> `billing` defaults to `monthly` (reserved projects default to `yearly`). For dynamic or short-lived workloads, set `billing = "hourly"` explicitly. Billing can be upgraded in place (`hourly` -> `monthly` -> `yearly`) but never downgraded.

